  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nThe initial round of showers and storms that was expected as this \nmid level shortwave coupled with a surface front push through the \narea, and round two is the cluster of showers and storms over \neastern South Dakota. This second round is expected to move across \nsouthern Minnesota this morning, arriving in our coverage area \ncloser to 5-6am and affecting the area along and south of the \nMinnesota River, with the best chance for more than showers being on \nthe southern edge of the line that is more likely to be in Iowa. All \nthis to say, we do not expect to see significant or widespread \nimpacts from the second round, primarily some showers and maybe \nrumbles of thunder that exits the area later this morning. The CAMs \nall have some form of cold air northwest flow isolated showers \nlingering throughout the day, and with a bit of mixed layer CAPE we \ncould see a few rumbles of thunder from any activity as well, \nalthough with unidirectional northwest flow, it is unlikely that \nanything more than a rumble or two results from it. The front will \nfully pass through during the day and leave us with weak high \npressure by this time tomorrow morning, resulting in quiet weather \nthrough Thursday when our next chance for showers and storms \narrives. Temperatures today and tomorrow will be in the 70s thanks \nto the weak subsidence from the weak high pressure as well as the \nmeager CAA from the northwest flow, returning to the 80s on Thursday \nas our next system arrives.\n\nSpeaking of, our next chance for rain will arrive on Thursday as a \nbroad area of weak forcing stretches south of a weak surface low in \ncentral Canada, with northwesterly flow aloft continuing. The \nsurface low looks to move southeast towards the northern tip of Lake \nSuperior by 12z Friday, and as it does our area finds itself between \nthe weak leading warm front and sagging cold front, resulting in \nchance for scattered showers and storms starting late Thursday \nmorning and continuing until the cold front moves through by midday \nFriday. The setup is rather interesting with a narrow upper level \njet streak left exit region arriving Friday morning coupled with a \nlow level jet, with the low level jet also within northwest flow \nwhich is a rather unique setup as typical nocturnal low level jet \nfeatures tend to favor southerly flow. One would think we will \nstruggle with moisture with this kind of setup, which is certainly a \nconsideration, however most of the guidance shows dew points in the \nlow to mid 60s with the ECMWF even suggesting upper 60s by Thursday \nevening. As long as our low level advection does not contain \nsignificant dry air, we should have enough moisture to force showers \nand storms. I would expect the forecast trend as we approach to \ngradually favor increasing PoPs and QPF, with ensemble probabilities \nshowing above 80% likelihood for at least a tenth of an inch across \nmost of the MPX coverage area by 7am Friday. The percentages get \nlower than 30% for a half inch or more, which seems realistic given \nthe pattern. As the cold front arrives on Friday, one last push of \nprecipitation will make way for more seasonal temperatures and weak \nhigh pressure to round out the 7 day period into early next week. \nThe longer range pattern continues to favor northwest flow aloft as \nthe ridge slides westwards, giving us potential for shortwaves to \nproduce precipitation, however also favoring a relatively low \nconfidence forecast at this time scale.",
